<Ui>
    <!--
        Print screen's UI half (decision 1487) — the centred "Screen Captured" line, and the
        `TakeScreenshot()` the SCREENSHOT binding runs.

        WHAT THE REFERENCE DOES, and why this file's ordering is the whole feature. Its
        Bindings.xml maps SCREENSHOT to `TakeScreenshot()`; that function hides this frame, THEN
        asks the engine to capture; the engine answers later with SCREENSHOT_SUCCEEDED or
        SCREENSHOT_FAILED, and only then does the text appear. So the message can never be in the
        picture, on either path:

          · the ordinary press — the outcome event lands frames after the readback, because the
            readback is asynchronous (`crate::screenshot`'s writer reports through a channel);
          · the double press inside the 1.5 s fade — the PREVIOUS shot's text is still on screen,
            and the Hide() below is what takes it off before the shutter.

        That second case is the one worth naming: it is the only way a "Screen Captured" could ever
        have reached a file, and it is why `TakeScreenshot` hides rather than just letting the fade
        run out. B261 asked for exactly this contract.

        PROVENANCE. The mechanism, the globals' names, the 1.5 s fade and the two strings are the
        reference's (its WorldFrame.xml/.lua carry them; the strings are GlobalStrings.lua l.3395-6,
        quoted as data like every other user-visible byte in this tree). The code is ours: the
        reference recomputes elapsed from `GetTime() - this.startTime` on every tick and ignores the
        elapsed its OnUpdate is handed — we accumulate the handed value instead, which is one clock
        read fewer per frame and one field fewer on the frame.

        SEATING. `parent="WorldFrame" setAllPoints="true"` with a CENTER-anchored string — the
        reference's own nesting (WorldFrame.xml l.42, inside WorldFrame's `<Frames>`), and it is
        load-bearing rather than decorative. That file's header states the law in one line:
        "Children of the world frame are visible even when the UI is turned off." benilla's
        WorldFrame is a stub (UIParent.xml) rather than the 3D layer, but it is a stub with the
        property that matters here — top-level, so nothing hides it.

        This read `parent="UIParent"` until 1757, on a note that argued the two seats are the same
        because both frames are full-screen and their CENTERs coincide. That was true of the
        GEOMETRY and blind to the rest: decision 1734 restored `SetFullScreenFrame`'s
        `UIParent:Hide()`, so under a fullscreen panel the UIParent seat is a hidden one — and the
        case that exposes it is this frame's own, because `CinematicFrame` answers the SCREENSHOT
        key by hand (`RunBinding`, 1701) precisely so a fly-by can be captured, and the
        confirmation for that capture could never appear. TOOLTIP strata is the reference's own —
        the confirmation has to be readable over whatever panel is open, and an explicit strata is
        not dragged down by a BACKGROUND-strata parent.
    -->
    <Script>
        <![CDATA[
        -- The two lines this frame can show, byte-exact from the 1.12 enUS GlobalStrings
        -- (interface.MPQ `GlobalStrings.lua`, line-cited) — user-visible text, so its bytes are the
        -- contract. Note the capital C: it is "Screen Captured", not "Screen captured".
        SCREENSHOT_SUCCESS = "Screen Captured";      -- GlobalStrings.lua:3396
        SCREENSHOT_FAILURE = "Screen Capture Failed";-- GlobalStrings.lua:3395

        -- Seconds the line takes to fade from full to gone. The reference's own value, and a
        -- GLOBAL there too — an addon may read or retune it, so it keeps the name.
        SCREENSHOT_STATUS_FADETIME = 1.5;

        -- The SCREENSHOT binding's body. Hide first, capture second: see the header.
        function TakeScreenshot()
            if ( ScreenshotStatus:IsVisible() ) then
                ScreenshotStatus:Hide();
            end
            Screenshot();
        end

        function ScreenshotStatus_OnLoad()
            this:RegisterEvent("SCREENSHOT_SUCCEEDED");
            this:RegisterEvent("SCREENSHOT_FAILED");
        end

        function ScreenshotStatus_OnEvent(event)
            if ( event == "SCREENSHOT_FAILED" ) then
                ScreenshotStatusText:SetText(SCREENSHOT_FAILURE);
            else
                ScreenshotStatusText:SetText(SCREENSHOT_SUCCESS);
            end
            -- The fade restarts from full on every outcome, so a second shot re-announces itself
            -- at full strength instead of inheriting whatever alpha the last one had faded to.
            this.fade = 0;
            this:SetAlpha(1.0);
            this:Show();
        end

        function ScreenshotStatus_OnUpdate(elapsed)
            local fade = (this.fade or 0) + (elapsed or 0);
            this.fade = fade;
            if ( fade >= SCREENSHOT_STATUS_FADETIME ) then
                this:Hide();
                return;
            end
            this:SetAlpha(1.0 - fade / SCREENSHOT_STATUS_FADETIME);
        end
        ]]>
    </Script>

    <Frame name="ScreenshotStatus" parent="WorldFrame" setAllPoints="true" frameStrata="TOOLTIP" hidden="true">
        <Layers>
            <Layer level="ARTWORK">
                <FontString name="ScreenshotStatusText" inherits="SystemFont">
                    <Anchors>
                        <Anchor point="CENTER" relativeTo="UIParent">
                            <Offset>
                                <AbsDimension x="0" y="0"/>
                            </Offset>
                        </Anchor>
                    </Anchors>
                </FontString>
            </Layer>
        </Layers>
        <Scripts>
            <OnLoad>
                ScreenshotStatus_OnLoad();
            </OnLoad>
            <OnEvent>
                ScreenshotStatus_OnEvent(event);
            </OnEvent>
            <OnUpdate>
                ScreenshotStatus_OnUpdate(arg1);
            </OnUpdate>
        </Scripts>
    </Frame>
</Ui>
